ents required of the Germans  the attack fails there's another attack  launched the next day it fails some men  there are a couple of battalions that  get across the river they are trapped  they get no more than a 500-yard  bridgehead across the river they are  under plunging fire and grazing fire  there were lots of wounds to the  buttocks from men trying to press as  close as they can to the ground and this  grazing fires coming across and and  hitting them in the butts essentially  they're recalled those who can there are  about twenty to hundred casualties at  the rapido River it's devastating it's  comparable to Omaha Beach in the  intensity of the casualties with the big  difference that the rapido gain nothing  Omaha Beach gained Normandy essentially  part of that invasion force the Texans  were so angry about it that they  conspired really right after the battle  to bring charges to insist on a  congressional investigation of General  Mark Clark who was the fifth Army  commander who had ordered the attack and  in indeed that happened after the war  there were congressional hearings both  the House and the Senate had hearings  congressional delegation from Texas  pushed it through Walker testified it  was mortifying to Clark who at this  point is that the Governor General  essentially in Austria after the war the  the findings by the army were that Clark  had acted within the bounds of his  authority and that his tactical  decisions were not to be second-guessed  after the war it still stings Clark's  name is not held in good odor in parts  of Texas as a consequence of this the  36th division redeems itself in a really  profound way at the very end of the  campaign going into Rome Walker was a  mining engineer as a college student at  Ojai  State and he knows topography and he  finds again their bottle up and he sees  a logging trail over Monte Artemisia  coming out of Anzio and he recognizes  that there's potential to this mining  this is a logging trail he 